.SS "double csmp::IsoparametricLinearPyramid::dN (\fBDenseMatrix\fP< DM_MIN > & DN2, const std::vector< double > & xyz)\fR [virtual]\fP"
Computes derivatives of shape functions at a point XY given in global coordinates within the element (also given in global coordinates)\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The element is used to obtain the global shape of the triangle and the interpolation function derivatives at the point XY are returned into the matrix M of dimensions rows = spatial dimensions x columns = nodes\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The interpolation-function derivative matrix is returned into the second method argument\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void csmp::IsoparametricLinearPyramid::dN (\fBDenseMatrix\fP< DM_MIN > & DN5)\fR [virtual]\fP"
Compute derivatives of shape functions at corresponding nodes with respect to the global coordinate system\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The element is used to obtain the global shape of the triangle and the interpolation function derivatives at each node are returned into the matrix M of dimensions rows = spatial dimensions x columns = nodes\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIDN5\fP The interpolation-function derivative matrix is returned into the second method argument\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::FiniteElement\fP\&.
.SS "double csmp::IsoparametricLinearPyramid::dN_AtBarycenter (\fBDenseMatrix\fP< DM_MIN > & B)\fR [virtual]\fP"
Method returns a vector with a size of 3 or 4, containing the consecutively ordered local node numbers of the side of the element which lies at the indicated model boundary\&. If the element is not on the model boundary an error is reported and the vector is initialized to unspecified\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The parent \fBElement\fP, the target boundary of the the \fBModel\fP of elements, and a Meschach vector which will hold the the local indices of the identified nodes (0\&.\&.\&.5)\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIfnids\fP The resulting local node id's are returned into the third method argument\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
While only the element knows which nodes are located at the mode boundary, the \fBFiniteElement\fP knows in which order these appear\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To assign Neumann boundary conditions with a PDE operator for surface integrals\&. 
.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::FiniteElement\fP\&.
.SS "double csmp::IsoparametricLinearPyramid::dN_AtIntegrationPoint (\fBDenseMatrix\fP< DM_MIN > & B, uint32_t gauss_point)\fR [virtual]\fP"

.IP "1." 4
Pre-multiplies local interpolation function derivative matrix, B, with node coordinates to obtain the Jacobian matrix, J\&.
.IP "2." 4
The Jacobian matrix J is inverted and multiplied with B to obtain the global interpolation function derivative matrix at the gauss point i (i=0\&.\&.\&.i=gauss points-1)\&.
.PP
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The first argument is a reference to the parent \fBElement\fP object, the second argument is the result matrix, the third argument indicates the gauss point at which the derivative matrix is computed and the fourth argument specifies the degrees of freedom per node, for which the B matrix shall be transformed\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIB\fP The global interpolation function derivative matrix is returned into the second method argument\&. The method also returns the determinant of the Jacobian matrix since it is often needed in integration procedures\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::FiniteElement\fP\&.
.SS "double csmp::IsoparametricLinearPyramid::dN_AtNode (\fBDenseMatrix\fP< DM_MIN > & B, uint32_t nd)\fR [virtual]\fP"

.IP "1." 4
Pre-multiplies local interpolation function derivative matrix, B, with node coordinates to obtain the Jacobian matrix, J at the desired node point\&.
.IP "2." 4
The Jacobian matrix J is inverted and multiplied with B to obtain the global interpolation function derivative matrix at the point i (i=0\&.\&.\&.i=gauss points-1)\&.
.PP
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The first argument is a reference to the parent \fBElement\fP object, the second argument is the result matrix, the third argument indicates the gauss point at which the derivative matrix is computed and the fourth argument specifies the degrees of freedom per node, for which the B matrix shall be transformed\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The global interpolation function derivative matrix is returned into the second method argument\&. The method also returns the determinant of the Jacobian matrix since it is often needed in integration procedures\&.
.RE
.PP
