.SS "void csmp::IsoparametricLinearPyramid::ExtrapolateIntegrationPointVariableToNodes (uint32_t nvars, const std::vector< double > & IVAR, std::vector< double > & NVAR) const\fR [virtual]\fP"
As the linear character of the element do not expect high accuracy, one integration point is enough and used in most of the applications (FE, V1 by Zienkewitch)\&.

.PP
Because in the local coordinate system, the pyramid is always straight sided, and the derivatives of the interpolation functions are bilinear, variable values computed at integration points can simply be extrapolated to the nodes\&. This involves the steps:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Compute the interpolation function coefficients for the pyramid which is defined by the one integration point\&.
.IP "2." 4
For each node point compute the values of the interpolation functions and use these to extrapolate the values of the variables at the nodes\&. 
.PP

.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::FiniteElement\fP\&.
.SS "double csmp::IsoparametricLinearPyramid::InnerRadius ()\fR [virtual]\fP"
This method attempts to calculate the inner radius of the curved-sided quad by talking 1/2 of the perimeter and dividing this measure by the area of the quad\&.

.PP
The procedure is empirically based giving a good match if the quadss are relatively even-sided and have straight edges\&. Requires further check for the case of the hexahedron, as it is directly borrowed from the Tetrahedron
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The parent element is queried for its node coordinates\&. 
.PP
